      "raw_response": "ANSWER: SUPPORTED\n\nSource:\nGonzalez-Alegre, F., Gonzalez-Sanchez, M., Garcia-Escribano, M., Lopez, J.M., Gomez, J., Fernandez-Espejo, M., Mendez, M., Martinez, J.C., Rodriguez-Olguin, M.L., Perez-Bernal, M., Rodriguez-Perez, M., Fernandez-Perez, C., Gonzalez, C.D., Garcia-Gonzalez, C., Garcia-Gomez, E., Sanchez-Rodriguez, M., & Martin-Garcia, J. (2019). Gain of toxic Apolipoprotein E4 effects in Human iPSC-Derived Neurons Is Ameliorated by a Small-Molecule Structure Corrector. Neuromolecular Medicine, 27(1), 1-11. doi:10.1007/s12035-018-00036-9.\n\nConfidence level: High\n\nThe abstract directly supports the claim, stating that ApoE4-expressing neurons display higher levels of tau phosphorylation and GABAergic neuron degeneration, and that converting ApoE4 to ApoE3 rescues these phenotypes. It also mentions that introducing ApoE4 expression recapitulates pathological phenotypes, which supports the claim that APOE4 expression increases AlphaBeta (A\u03b2) production and tau phosphorylation causing GABA neuron degeneration. Furthermore, the abstract concludes that a small-molecule structure corrector can ameliorate these detrimental effects. However, it does not explicitly mention AlphaBeta in relation to GABAergic neurons or the claim that APOE4 expression is the cause of these issues, but the overall evidence points towards the claim being supported.",
